[48;2;223;239;233m[38;2;31;111;92m[1m Q1 [22m[38;2;36;31;27m You loved your son, Marcus. You loved him the way other people need air. His father, Gerald, died when Marcus was nine, and after that it was just the two of you in the house on Fenwick Road, and you made sure it stayed that way. [0m

[48;2;240;235;229m[38;2;168;55;27m[1m Q2 Q5 [22m[38;2;36;31;27m When Marcus was eighteen, an acceptance letter came from Oberlin. You told him it was a mistake — that the financial aid office had called and rescinded it. You had never spoken to anyone at Oberlin. You burned the letter in the kitchen sink. You told yourself a boy like Marcus, a boy that sensitive, would be eaten alive out there. You were protecting him. That was the lie, and you told it so long you forgot it was one. [0m

[48;2;240;235;229m[38;2;168;55;27m[1m Q2 Q3 [22m[38;2;36;31;27m You told yourself the same lie when you called his employer, Denny at the hardware store, to say Marcus had panic attacks and shouldn't be left alone with customers. When you told his girlfriend, Rachel, that Marcus had said terrible things about her, which he hadn't. Rachel left. Marcus stayed. He was forty-one before he understood any of it, and when he did, he moved to Phoenix without giving you the address. [0m

[48;2;222;233;244m[38;2;44;90;138m[1m Q4 [22m[38;2;36;31;27m To the neighbors, to Pastor Wilkes, to everyone at St. Agnes, you were the woman who sacrificed everything for an ungrateful son. You said he abandoned you. You said you had given him your whole life. [0m

