[48;2;223;239;233m[38;2;31;111;92m[1m Q1 [22m[38;2;36;31;27m You loved your daughter, Lucy. You loved her the way you loved the front garden: photographed, trimmed, shown to the PTA. Lucy was tall for twelve, first chair violin at Kingsley Middle School, the girl other mothers asked about at the grocery store. [0m

[48;2;240;235;229m[38;2;168;55;27m[1m Q2 Q3 [22m[38;2;36;31;27m At fourteen Lucy stopped eating. You noticed — of course you noticed. You bought her a smoothie maker, signed her up for tennis, told your sister Karen that Lucy had just gotten "so disciplined." Karen asked if she should see someone. You said Lucy was fine. You said it until Lucy was sixteen and had a seizure in the kitchen while you were steaming the curtains for a dinner party. [0m

[48;2;246;237;214m[38;2;138;106;18m[1m Q3 [22m[38;2;36;31;27m You told the paramedics she'd been fine that morning. You told David, your husband, that she hid it well, that no mother could have known. You told the psychiatrist at Willow Creek it must have started at school — maybe that gym teacher, Mr. Barrett, you'd always had doubts. [0m

[48;2;247;227;220m[38;2;168;55;27m[1m Q2 [22m[38;2;36;31;27m Lucy wrote a note. Four pages. Your name was on the second page, in her pointed handwriting: "Everything is a performance for her. Even me." [0m

[48;2;247;223;238m[38;2;147;48;107m[1m Q5 [22m[38;2;36;31;27m You read it in the hospital waiting room and burned it in the sink with the lighter David kept for the grill. You told him there was no note. You told yourself you were protecting her memory. [0m

[48;2;247;227;220m[38;2;168;55;27m[1m Q2 [22m[38;2;36;31;27m Lucy came home thinner, quieter, managed. She went to bed on a Tuesday in March and didn't wake up on Wednesday. She was seventeen. [0m

