[48;2;223;239;233m[38;2;31;111;92m[1m Q1 [22m[38;2;36;31;27m I loved my daughter, Lucía. She became a nurse because of me — used to do her homework at the station by my elbow, and when she pinned on her badge at 22, she said, "I want to be you." [0m

[48;2;240;235;229m[38;2;31;111;92m[1m Q1 Q5 [22m[38;2;36;31;27m I also loved the thing I could do. When a patient was past saving and the family was cruel with hope, I gave them quiet. Morphine, up past the line. I did it eleven times in four years. I told myself I was mercy. I told the families nothing. [0m

[48;2;247;227;220m[38;2;168;55;27m[1m Q2 [22m[38;2;36;31;27m Then came Ramón Delgado, 38, recovering from a gallbladder surgery gone septic. His numbers were turning. His wife, Ana Delgado, had begged me that morning — "do everything" — and I resented her for it, because I had already decided I knew better. I pulled the wrong chart into the wrong conclusion and I gave him the dose. He was breathing on his own the next week, was my understanding. Except he never got the week. [0m

[48;2;247;227;220m[38;2;168;55;27m[1m Q2 [22m[38;2;36;31;27m Lucía caught me adjusting the pump on a night she'd picked up an extra shift. She didn't report me for three days — three days of watching me eat dinner, laugh at her stories about her own patients. Then she filed the report with her own name on it. Her first year of practice, and she burned her mother with it. I lost my license. Nurses who'd worked with me for a decade stopped saying my name in the break room. [0m

[48;2;246;237;214m[38;2;138;106;18m[1m Q3 [22m[38;2;36;31;27m The lie I told everyone, including Lucía, at the hearing: that Ramón was the first. That it was only ever mercy. [0m

